{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["There are some grammarians who said that the heh of the question-word does not take a dagesh after one of the seven [letter] kings, but it does occur with mobile sheva, and the heh is open [with pataḥ] because it is impossible for two shevin to combine. And because they found a yod in הייטב with a dagesh, they said that this heh is the definite article, like the heh in השבה עם נעמי, הנמצאו פה העיר, ההוללה, ואלה ההי״ן, in place of אשר הנה כן, the meaning of הייטב. And further they said that ואכלתי is a past-tense verb, and if it were future it would be milra, like ודברתי אל הנביאים, ודברתי אתך. And the meaning: for the sons who were burned today offered their sin-offering and their burnt-offering, for the calf and the ram were on behalf of Aaron and his sons." "<b>ותקראנה אותי כאלה.</b> Meaning troubles or anxieties over the death of the sons, and because of this I could not eat the entire sin-offering; I ate from it only what was ייטב in the eyes of HaShem, and the meaning: what would bring me into liability. And according to the translators [whose view is] that the heh of הייטב is for a question — and it is a strange word — also according to the interpretation that I mentioned it is strange, for we do not find in all of Scripture a heh of the definite article with a future-tense verb. And the word <b>ואכלתי חטאת.</b> As if he said: if I had eaten a sin-offering. We also find future-tense words that are milra: פן אורש, וגנבתי. And the meaning: for I was an onen, and an onen does not eat a sin-offering, in the sense of לא אכלתי באוני."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The text 'שבעת מלכים' likely refers to the seven letters בגד כפרת that take beged-kefet dagesh; 'kings' is left literally as it appears."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:09:28.630842209Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}